moscas?"},{"expresion":"to cut corners","linea_aprox":19,"coach_intro":"David desmenuza «to cut corners»: hacer algo por el camino fácil y barato, saltándose pasos importantes para ahorrar tiempo o esfuerzo. Casi siempre es metafórico y con tono de reproche, pero Fred Lorz lo hizo literal: se saltó once millas de maratón subido a un auto.","coach_ejemplo":"\"They cut corners on the construction, and now the roof leaks.\" — Recortaron pasos en la construcción, y ahora el techo gotea.","coach_cierre":"Piensa en algo que hiciste a las carreras y te salió caro: ¿cómo lo contarías en inglés usando «cut corners»?"},{"expresion":"to hit the wall","linea_aprox":26,"coach_intro":"«To hit the wall» —chocar contra el muro— es el momento en que la energía se acaba por completo y el cuerpo o la mente se niegan a seguir. Nació en el mundo del maratón, como le pasó a Thomas Hicks alucinando a millas de la meta, pero hoy se usa para cualquier agotamiento: estudiar, trabajar, una semana interminable.","coach_ejemplo":"\"After six hours of studying, I hit the wall and couldn't read another page.\" — Después de seis horas de estudio, choqué contra el muro y no pude leer ni una página más.","coach_cierre":"¿A qué hora del día sueles chocar tú contra el muro, y qué haces para recuperarte?"}],"onda":[1,0.872,1,0.876,0.969,0.936,0.877,0.886,0.945,0.832,0.921,0.861,0.738,0.856,0.953,0.872,0.967,0.911,0.854,0.911,0.617,1,0.776,1,0.956,0.922,0.967,0.997,0.751,0.89,0.851,0.901,0.855,0.957,0.818,0.909,1,0.939,0.926,0.905,0.919,0.902,0.816,0.913,0.918,0.82,0.865,0.865,0.857,0.913,0.705,0.938,0.873,0.915,0.911,0.831,0.997,0.821,0.812,0.961,0.82,0.733,0.861,0.834,0.846,0.871,0.887,0.785,0.806,0.813,0.874,0.608,0.954,0.942,0.878,0.982,0.906,0.93,0.883,0.894,0.702,0.808,0.942,0.813,0.954,0.93,0.65,0.863,0.702,0.948,0.956,0.919,0.915,0.876,0.876,0.782,0.817,0.912,0.762,0.914,0.837,0.982,0.811,0.865,0.807,0.81,0.738,0.791,0.87,0.812,0.923,0.913,0.881,0.786,0.806,0.925,0.749,0.827,0.676,0.796,0.944,0.